# EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR
#~
Multiplying by a literal zero must still evaluate the other operand.

The optimizer rewrites `x * 0` to 0. When the other operand is not a lone load
(here a division) it is still evaluated and its value dropped, so a division by
zero inside it traps as it does at -opt s0. A rewrite that deleted the operand
along with the multiply would print 0 and exit normally.

The harness passes this only on a non-zero exit WITH output (the trap message).
~#

class ArithMulZeroTrap {
  function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
    # read from an array so the divisor is not a compile-time constant
    zeros := Int->New[1];
    z := zeros[0];
    x := (5 / z) * 0;
    x->PrintLine();
  }
}
